# Rule E-binop 

Then we have as IH:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{A} \triangleright\left\langle\left(\mathrm{e}_{1}\right)\right\rangle_{c} \downarrow\left\langle\left(\mathrm{n}_{1}\right)\right\rangle_{c}: \mathrm{t}_{1} \\
& \mathrm{~A} \triangleright\left\langle\left(\mathrm{e}_{2}\right)\right\rangle_{c} \downarrow\left\langle\left(\mathrm{n}_{1}\right)\right\rangle_{c}: \mathrm{t}_{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

and $\left\langle\left(\mathrm{e}_{1}+\mathrm{e}_{2}\right)\right\rangle_{c}=\left\langle\left(\mathrm{e}_{1}\right)\right\rangle_{c}+\left\langle\left(\mathrm{e}_{2}\right)\right\rangle_{c}$.
Follows from IH and Rule E-binop.

## V. 1 Union of States

The states $\Sigma_{x y}$ are defined as the union of the state of its parts

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \Sigma_{x y}::=\Sigma_{x} \cup \Sigma_{y} \\
& \Phi_{x y}::=\Phi_{x} \cup \Phi_{y}
\end{aligned}
$$

# AA The problem with SLH and $J$ 

In the paper we claimed that lifting the security guarantees of $\llbracket \llbracket \rrbracket_{\mathrm{B}}^{S S L H}$ and $\llbracket \llbracket \rrbracket_{\mathrm{B}}^{L S L H}$ to a combination including $\bigcap_{J}$ is not possible.

Now, let us take an example program $p$ written in C that is secure for $\bigcap_{J}: \bigcap_{J} \vdash p:$ RSS:

```
int array1[160];
int array2[256 * 512];
unsigned int array1_size = 16;
```

, Vol. 1, No. 1, Article . Publication date: March 2025.

```
void leak(int idx) {
    int x = array1[idx];
    int y = array2[x * 4096];
}
void main() {
    int x = 0;
    if (x != 0){
        int x2 = array1[idx];
        //int y2 = array2[x2 * 4096];
        static const void* table[] = {&&winter, &&spring,
            &&summer, &&fall);
        goto *table[x2];
        winter:
            return;
        spring:
            return;
        summer:
            return;
        fall:
            return;
        return;
    }
}
```

This program is secure because the indirect jump is never executed because it is guarded by the branch.

After SLH the program looks like this using clang with option -mspeculative-load-hardening.

